# =============================================================================
#  Decay over orders of magnitude    A --k--> (gone)
# =============================================================================
#  A single first-order decay, A(t) = A0 exp(-k t), started high (A0 = 1000) and
#  followed long enough that A falls through three orders of magnitude (~1000 down
#  to ~1). The measurement noise is MULTIPLICATIVE -- a constant 15% of whatever
#  the value is -- so in ABSOLUTE terms the early large points carry huge scatter
#  (+-150 near t=0) and the late small points carry tiny scatter (+-0.2 near the
#  tail).
#
#  That is the setup where the CHOICE OF OBJECTIVE matters (lesson 35):
#     * sos (absolute error) is dominated by the large early points and their big
#       absolute residuals -> it under-uses the informative small-magnitude tail
#       and the recovered k is dragged off.
#     * norm_sos (RELATIVE error: each residual divided by the value) weights every
#       point equally regardless of magnitude -> k is recovered.
#
#  Only k is fitted; A0 is fixed/known.
# =============================================================================

begin model

begin parameters
  k   0.4    # decay rate [1/day]  <- FITTED
  A0  1000   # initial amount [AU] (fixed/known)
end parameters

begin molecule types
  A()
end molecule types

begin seed species
  A()  A0
end seed species

begin observables
  Molecules  Obs_A  A()
end observables

begin reaction rules
  A_decay: A() -> 0  k
end reaction rules

end model
